<h2>Why the seat question reached Article 136</h2>
<p>Section 11(6) petitions go to "the High Court" — but which one? Since the seat doctrine crystallised, the answer has been the High Court exercising supervisory jurisdiction over the juridical seat. The appellant resisted the Delhi appointment on precisely that ground, and when the High Court proceeded nonetheless, the special leave jurisdiction under Article 136 became the only corrective. The Supreme Court granted leave and decided the appeal on the clause\'s construction — a reminder that seat objections are jurisdictional and survive all the way up.</p>

<h2>The analytical sequence</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Count the places</strong> — the three-condition test treats a solitary named place, with proceedings anchored there and no contrary indicia, as the seat. Clause 32 named two places, so no automatic designation arose.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Read the whole contract</strong> — with the shortcut unavailable, Clause 38\'s grant of exclusive jurisdiction to Goa courts and Clause 32.5\'s location of hearings in Goa had to be read together with Clause 32.4\'s reference to Delhi.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Harmonise, don\'t delete</strong> — the Court read the clauses so that none became redundant: Goa is the juridical seat; Delhi, a venue where sittings may conveniently occur.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Jurisdictional consequence</strong> — supervisory jurisdiction follows the seat; the Delhi High Court\'s Section 11 order was set aside, and the parties were relegated to the jurisdictional High Court.</div>
</div>

<h2>The drafting lesson that will not retire</h2>
<p>Every year of Indian arbitration reports contains a seat-venue casualty, and the pattern is always the same: a clause that names one city for "arbitration", another for "proceedings", and a third provision handing jurisdiction to courts somewhere else. The fix costs one sentence: <em>"The seat of arbitration shall be [city]; hearings may be held at any convenient venue."</em> Paired with a jurisdiction clause pointing to the same city\'s courts, that sentence eliminates the entire genre of litigation this judgment represents. Transactional lawyers reviewing franchise, distribution and construction agreements should treat a two-city dispute clause as a defect to be cured at signature, not a nuance to be argued later.</p>

<h2>Where this fits in SLP practice</h2>
<p>For Supreme Court practice, the decision illustrates a category in which special leave is realistically granted: a pure question of jurisdiction, fully documentary, where the High Court\'s order would otherwise channel an entire arbitration into the supervision of the wrong forum. No disputed facts, no concurrent findings — the classic profile of an SLP that converts into a civil appeal and succeeds. Litigants weighing a challenge to a Section 11 appointment should note, equally, what the Court did not do: it expressed no view on the arbitrability or merits of the underlying franchise dispute, confining itself to the seat.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>Neutral citation 2026 INSC 1109; the judgment is reportable. The appointment question now lies before the High Court with supervisory jurisdiction over Goa, and the arbitration agreement itself stands unaffected.</p>
</div>
